Why non-commercial
------------------

The weights are not more permissive by choice. They are a derived work of the
training data, and one of the four training sources carries a non-commercial
restriction that flows through to anything trained on it:

    PolyU Real-World Noisy Images Dataset
    Copyright (c) 2018, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University
    "Any redistribution, use, or modification is done solely for
     non-commercial purposes."

PolyU supplied 35% of the training mixture for the released checkpoint (the
mixture was SIDD 0.25 / PolyU 0.35 / synthetic 0.40). It was not an incidental
ingredient: PolyU was the only source of *real* spatially-correlated camera
noise, which is what made the model work on real photographs at all — training
on white noise alone left the model unable to tell correlated noise blobs from
structure. See `docs/architecture.md`.

The other training sources are permissive: SIDD is MIT, and the synthetic
degradations are generated by this project's own code.

If you need commercially usable weights
---------------------------------------

Retrain without PolyU. The code to do so is Apache-2.0 and complete: adjust
`data.mixture` in a training config to drop `polyu` and redistribute its weight
between `sidd` and `synthetic`, then train per the instructions in `README.md`.
Expect a quality cost — removing the only real correlated-noise source is a
material change, and the result would need re-validating against the gates in
`docs/nagi_v2_parameter_audit.md`. Weights you produce that way are yours, and
are not covered by this file.

